1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,300
So, Wei, tell me where
the proposal has got to.

2
00:00:04,234 --> 00:00:08,800
Well, I started out wanting to compare
bees in the countryside and in towns,

3
00:00:09,450 --> 00:00:10,720
but I've narrowed it down.

4
00:00:11,160 --> 00:00:13,320
I'm now only looking at urban areas.

5
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:15,980
Sensible. And your central question?

6
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:19,100
Climate and habitat loss
are obviously important,

7
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:22,400
but what I really want to
find out is whether pesticides,

8
00:00:23,045 --> 00:00:25,580
especially the chemicals
people buy for their gardens,

9
00:00:25,980 --> 00:00:27,360
are reducing bee numbers.

10
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:29,380
Where will you be collecting data?

11
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:33,980
I'll compare parks with gardens,
public green space against private.

12
00:00:34,435 --> 00:00:36,654
Something else that
will affect numbers is how

13
00:00:36,678 --> 00:00:38,812
many kinds of flower
there are at each site.

14
00:00:39,374 --> 00:00:43,260
I'd measure flower diversity as well, or
you won't be able to separate the effects.

15
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:44,820
I hadn't thought of that.

16
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:47,000
How long will you be collecting data for?

17
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:49,680
I'd thought about six
months over the summer,

18
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:54,640
but bees behave so differently through the
year that I've gone for a 12-month period.

19
00:00:55,150 --> 00:00:56,740
Because you'll be on council land,

20
00:00:56,889 --> 00:00:59,680
you'll need sign-off from the
ethics committee before you start,

21
00:01:00,059 --> 00:01:01,660
not just permission from the council.

22
00:01:02,009 --> 00:01:03,360
The application's half-written.

23
00:01:03,750 --> 00:01:04,760
And your main method?

24
00:01:05,099 --> 00:01:06,340
I considered traps,

25
00:01:06,609 --> 00:01:08,200
but I don't want to kill any bees.

26
00:01:08,869 --> 00:01:10,680
So I'll do timed bee counts.

27
00:01:11,130 --> 00:01:15,000
I stand at a fixed point for 10
minutes and count every bee that lands.

28
00:01:15,330 --> 00:01:16,330
Anything qualitative?

29
00:01:16,779 --> 00:01:18,440
I'm going to talk to local beekeepers.

30
00:01:19,069 --> 00:01:21,540
Some of them have been
watching their hives for decades.

31
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:24,731
For the counts, follow a
standard survey protocol so

32
00:01:24,755 --> 00:01:27,572
that other people can
compare their results with yours.

33
00:01:27,904 --> 00:01:30,120
I'll find out which one
is most widely used.

34
00:01:30,564 --> 00:01:32,280
One big risk is the weather.

35
00:01:32,864 --> 00:01:36,040
On a cold or windy day,
bee activity drops sharply,

36
00:01:36,459 --> 00:01:38,640
so record the conditions on every visit.

37
00:01:38,964 --> 00:01:40,460
Temperature and wind speed, then.

38
00:01:40,764 --> 00:01:42,780
And keep a detailed field notebook.

39
00:01:43,289 --> 00:01:46,060
Not just the numbers, but
anything unusual you notice.

40
00:01:46,609 --> 00:01:48,380
I hadn't really thought about funding.

41
00:01:48,899 --> 00:01:51,040
The department offers
a small research grant,

42
00:01:51,210 --> 00:01:52,720
and the deadline is coming up soon.

43
00:01:53,409 --> 00:01:55,360
Will you be sharing any
findings before the end?

44
00:01:56,060 --> 00:01:59,440
There's a conference in spring
where I can present my early results.

45
00:01:59,889 --> 00:02:03,160
For the literature review,
some students go back 30 years.

46
00:02:03,649 --> 00:02:05,580
Concentrate on studies from the last 10.

47
00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:06,745
Okay.

48
00:02:06,769 --> 00:02:11,360
One weakness I often see in early
drafts is claims that are far too broad.

49
00:02:12,020 --> 00:02:15,740
Six sites in one city can't tell you
about bees across the whole country.

50
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:17,080
I'll be careful.

51
00:02:17,330 --> 00:02:18,960
When is the full proposal due?

52
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:20,150
Wait.

53
00:02:20,174 --> 00:02:22,220
It was originally the end of the month,

54
00:02:22,490 --> 00:02:25,080
but the department has
extended it to the end of term.

55
00:02:25,490 --> 00:02:26,600
Are you doing a trial run?

56
00:02:26,900 --> 00:02:31,640
I wanted to do a pilot in summer,
but it'll have to be in autumn now.

57
00:02:31,940 --> 00:02:33,380
Have you got the equipment you need?

58
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:36,500
I've got a GPS, but
nothing to record images.

59
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:38,440
The department can lend you a camera.

60
00:02:38,850 --> 00:02:40,800
And how will you analyse the results?

61
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:43,500
I'll use statistical software,

62
00:02:44,025 --> 00:02:45,800
the package we learned on my stats module.

63
00:02:46,210 --> 00:02:47,860
How long will the final report be?

64
00:02:48,755 --> 00:02:51,160
The guidelines say 30 to 50 pages,

65
00:02:51,620 --> 00:02:53,320
so I'm aiming for about 40.

66
00:02:53,670 --> 00:02:55,440
That sounds like a solid plan, Wei.

